1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:01,320
Every 3D print

2
00:00:01,320 --> 00:00:02,507
starts as a single

3
00:00:02,507 --> 00:00:04,155
thread of plastic.

4
00:00:04,155 --> 00:00:05,305
First, software

5
00:00:05,305 --> 00:00:06,130
called a slicer

6
00:00:06,130 --> 00:00:07,467
cuts your 3D model

7
00:00:07,467 --> 00:00:08,193
into hundreds

8
00:00:08,193 --> 00:00:09,292
of thin layers,

9
00:00:09,292 --> 00:00:10,555
and turns each one

10
00:00:10,555 --> 00:00:11,330
into a path

11
00:00:11,330 --> 00:00:12,043
of coordinates

12
00:00:12,043 --> 00:00:13,315
called G-code.

13
00:00:13,315 --> 00:00:14,652
The printer feeds filament

14
00:00:14,652 --> 00:00:15,902
into the hot end.

15
00:00:15,902 --> 00:00:16,652
Drive gears

16
00:00:16,652 --> 00:00:17,752
push it forward,

17
00:00:17,752 --> 00:00:18,590
a heater melts it

18
00:00:18,590 --> 00:00:20,490
at around 200 degrees,

19
00:00:20,490 --> 00:00:21,127
and a nozzle

20
00:00:21,127 --> 00:00:23,377
about 0.4 millimeters wide

21
00:00:23,377 --> 00:00:24,737
squeezes it out.

22
00:00:24,737 --> 00:00:25,887
Motors move the nozzle

23
00:00:25,887 --> 00:00:27,575
across X and Y,

24
00:00:27,575 --> 00:00:28,875
tracing the outer walls

25
00:00:28,875 --> 00:00:30,300
first, then filling

26
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:31,000
the inside

27
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:31,675
with a pattern

28
00:00:31,675 --> 00:00:32,972
called infill.

29
00:00:32,972 --> 00:00:33,485
When a layer

30
00:00:33,485 --> 00:00:34,260
is done,

31
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:35,435
the bed drops,

32
00:00:35,435 --> 00:00:36,847
or the nozzle rises,

33
00:00:36,847 --> 00:00:37,697
by a fraction

34
00:00:37,697 --> 00:00:38,910
of a millimeter.

35
00:00:38,910 --> 00:00:39,535
The next layer

36
00:00:39,535 --> 00:00:40,797
is laid on top,

37
00:00:40,797 --> 00:00:41,873
and heat fuses it

38
00:00:41,873 --> 00:00:43,107
to the one below.

39
00:00:43,107 --> 00:00:43,907
A fan cools

40
00:00:43,907 --> 00:00:44,507
each line

41
00:00:44,507 --> 00:00:45,082
so it holds

42
00:00:45,082 --> 00:00:46,020
its shape.

43
00:00:46,020 --> 00:00:46,957
Steep overhangs

44
00:00:46,957 --> 00:00:48,682
need temporary supports,

45
00:00:48,682 --> 00:00:49,507
because plastic

46
00:00:49,507 --> 00:00:50,370
can't be printed

47
00:00:50,370 --> 00:00:51,680
on thin air.

48
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:52,830
Layer by layer,

49
00:00:52,830 --> 00:00:54,017
line by line,

50
00:00:54,017 --> 00:00:54,730
until a solid

51
00:00:54,730 --> 00:00:56,192
object appears.

52
00:00:56,192 --> 00:00:57,442
That's FDM.

53
00:00:57,442 --> 00:00:58,855
Follow Futurix3D

54
00:00:58,855 --> 00:00:59,847
for more.
